WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.952
hello-jvm now has two projects.

2
00:00:04.952 --> 00:00:09.092
app is the web service, and it uses the library greeting.

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.192
Now try an experiment.

4
00:00:11.192 --> 00:00:16.609
app asks for a library directly: commons-lang3, version 3.17.0.

5
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:19.467
And greeting uses commons-text inside.

6
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:21.753
It declares it as implementation.

7
00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:25.233
But libraries have dependencies of their own.

8
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:30.314
The metadata file of commons-text lists commons-lang3, version 3.18.0.

9
00:00:30.314 --> 00:00:32.056
So that comes along too.

10
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:34.410
These are transitive dependencies.

11
00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.317
In a real service, they often outnumber the ones you declared.

12
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:42.401
Now two paths ask for different versions of the same library.

13
00:00:42.401 --> 00:00:43.859
Which one does app get?

14
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:46.386
Gradle settles this with one rule.

15
00:00:46.386 --> 00:00:48.091
The highest version wins.

16
00:00:48.091 --> 00:00:50.956
Your own declaration gets no extra weight.

17
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:56.427
The bet: the library that asks for the newer version may need something only it has.

18
00:00:56.427 --> 00:01:00.247
Code written for the older one usually runs on the newer too.

19
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:02.776
This surprises people who know Maven.

20
00:01:02.776 --> 00:01:05.835
There, the declaration nearest your project wins.

21
00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:09.853
The dependencies report shows it with an arrow.

22
00:01:09.853 --> 00:01:13.397
3.17.0 was asked for, and 3.18.0 was chosen.

23
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.050
But which classpath?

24
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:19.834
A classpath is the list of jars the compiler, or the JVM, can see.

25
00:01:19.834 --> 00:01:23.241
app has one for compiling, and one for running.

26
00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:28.075
The runtime classpath gets everything the program needs to run.

27
00:01:28.075 --> 00:01:32.611
greeting, commons-text, and one copy of commons-lang3: 3.18.0.

28
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:35.139
The compile classpath is different.

29
00:01:35.139 --> 00:01:36.324
greeting is there.

30
00:01:36.324 --> 00:01:41.067
But commons-text is not, because greeting declared it as implementation.

31
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.334
Without commons-text, nothing here asks for 3.18.0.

32
00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:48.341
So the compile classpath keeps 3.17.0.

33
00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:51.347
Each classpath is resolved on its own.

34
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:54.034
So app cannot use commons-text itself.

35
00:01:54.034 --> 00:01:58.385
Import a class from it, and the compiler says unresolved reference.

36
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.127
Change that one line from implementation to api.

37
00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:05.837
Now commons-text joins app's compile classpath too.

38
00:02:05.837 --> 00:02:10.027
And it brings 3.18.0 along, so the highest wins here as well.

39
00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:15.334
Use api only when a type from that library appears in your public functions.

40
00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.884
Then every caller needs it to compile.

41
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.946
Otherwise, make implementation your default.

42
00:02:20.946 --> 00:02:23.340
It keeps a library's insides private.

43
00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:25.252
It also saves build time.

44
00:02:25.252 --> 00:02:31.452
When you upgrade an implementation dependency, the projects that use yours do not recompile.

45
00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:35.263
Every library brings its own dependencies.

46
00:02:35.263 --> 00:02:38.090
When two versions meet, the highest wins.

47
00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:45.400
And each classpath is resolved on its own: implementation stays hidden when callers compile, api does not.
